{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Elitzur son of Shedei'ur.</b> Perhaps it alludes to Reuben, whom HaShem pardoned for the sin recorded in the Torah (Genesis 35:22) ״וַיִּשְׁכַּב״ etc., and applied balm to heal his affliction. This is what it says: <b>eli</b> means 'my El'; <b>tzur</b> in the sense of (Jeremiah 8:22) ״הַצֳּרִי אֵין בְּגִלְעָד״; <b>son of Shedei'ur</b> here alludes to the matter recorded in the Torah, ״וַיִּשְׁכַּב״ etc. And the balm is that immediately after the statement ״וַיִּשְׁכַּב״ etc., it says ״וַיִּהְיוּ בְנֵי יַעֲקֹב שְׁנֵים עָשָׂר״ — by this the wound was healed. And according to the words of our Sages, of blessed memory, who said (Shabbat 55b) 'whoever says Reuben sinned' etc. — these are the very things that HaShem showed through the juxtaposition of ״וַיִּהְיוּ בְנֵי יַעֲקֹב״ etc."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:38.563366440Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}